  "# Kotoba — Y Combinator application draft (F26 late / W27)\n\n**Status:** draft, ready to paste except OWNER-FILL fields\n**As-of:** 2026-08-13\n**Form:** live YC apply form (company / progress / idea / equity / video)\n**Submit:** owner only. Do not submit until the 1-minute video URL exists.\n**Legal entity:** not formed yet. Owner will form a Delaware C-Corp later (ADR-2608139970).\n\nDo not paste sister-product metrics as Kotoba traction. Verified external revenue of the language = 0.\n\n---\n\n## Founders\n\nTwo founders: Jun Kawasaki (Tokyo; language/compiler) and Ichihara (marketing, sales, bizdev; invited, profile still incomplete at i@awai.network). Known more than 15 years; university senpai; ran a company together when Jun was 20. Planned equity Jun 90% / Ichihara 10%.\n\nHow long have the founders known one another?\nIchihara is my university senpai. We have known each other for more than 15 years and have met in person. We ran a company together when I was 20. Ichihara is co-founder on this application (invite: i@awai.network; profile still incomplete).\n\nWho writes code, or does other technical work on your product? Was any of it done by a non-founder?\nJun Kawasaki writes the language, compiler (Wasm + native x86-64/AArch64), and the public site. AI coding tools (Cursor, Claude) are used as editors. No outsourced engineering team. Ichihara is co-founder for marketing, sales, and bizdev, not compiler authorship.\n\nAre you looking for a cofounder?\nNo. Ichihara is already invited as co-founder on this application. Not looking for an additional cofounder through YC matching.\n\nFounder bio (paste into the founder profile):\nI am the technical founder in Tokyo (CEO/CTO, 90%). Ichihara is co-founder for marketing, sales, and bizdev (10%); profile still incomplete. I designed Kotoba, a capability-safe language for programs written by AI, and I wrote the compiler that emits WebAssembly and native x86-64/AArch64 machine code. I use it in production: 33 inference decision cores on our own fleet compile through that path to machine code (ADR-2608061000). I also operate sister products (kotobase.net, itonami.cloud) under separate companies; those are not this application and have $0 verified external revenue. Before Kotoba I built Gftd Japan as a security-focused operating company. At DEF CON 33 (Las Vegas, August 2025) I placed 2nd among one-person teams and 9th overall in the Car Hacking Village CTF (about 40 teams; https://prtimes.jp/main/html/rd/p/000000005.000129278.html). The through-line is the same: software should not receive ambient authority just because it compiled.\n\nOWNER-FILL: education, age, phone, LinkedIn, Twitter/X, personal email if not hello@gftd.co.jp, GitHub: https://github.com/com-junkawasaki (correct). Do not use github.com/junkawasaki on the YC bio..\n\nPlease tell us about a time you most successfully hacked some (non-computer) system to your advantage.\nOWNER-FILL. Do not invent. A usable shape: one system, one constraint, one action, one concrete outcome. Not a school grade appeal. Not a programming story.\n\n---\n\n## Founder video\n\nPlease record a one minute video introducing the founder(s).\nOWNER-FILL URL (YouTube unlisted). No demo, no slides, no reading a script. Phone or Zoom is enough.\n\nTalking points (do not read aloud):\n- 0:00 I am Jun Kawasaki. This is Ichihara, co-founder. I built Kotoba; Ichihara does marketing, sales, and bizdev.\n- 0:08 AI now writes most new software, but it runs with the same authority as a trusted human.\n- 0:20 Kotoba is a language where an effect you were not granted cannot be written at all. If it compiles, it's contained.\n- 0:35 The compiler already emits Wasm and native machine code, with no LLVM. We use it ourselves on production inference cores.\n- 0:48 No external customers yet. Two founders, 15+ years. Applying so we can sell this as one product, not a pile of infrastructure.\n\nDemo video (optional, separate field): every step below was executed end to end on 2026-08-17 and works today, so this is a recording job, not a build job. Do not substitute the founder video with a screencast.\n\n1. An agent writes code that reaches for the network.\n2. `amu compile` refuses: `:kotoba/admission-denied — capability policy denies required effects`. No binary exists.\n3. Grant exactly one capability in a policy file: `{:allow #{[:cap/call 7]}}`.\n4. It compiles. 331 bytes of Wasm, 1.8 seconds. `main()` returns.\n5. `amu sign-output-set` then `amu verify-output-set --trust` → `:publisher-authenticated true`.\n6. Change one byte of the artifact and verify again → `ok false, \"output set is not committed\"`.\n\n---\n\n## Taglines\n\nThe 50-character form field is a description and is scanned in seconds; the tagline is what a partner repeats afterwards. Do not make one string do both jobs.\n\n| Slot | Line |\n|---|---|\n| Banner | Safety First — never on its own; the mechanism line always sits directly under it |\n| Investor / non-technical | We don't sandbox unsafe code. We refuse to build it. |\n| **Primary technical tagline** | **If it compiles, it's contained.** |\n| Lisp / Hacker News | A Lisp with no eval. |\n| Practitioner | Review the grant, not the diff. |\n| Manifesto | Authority is a type. |\n| Evidence, said immediately after any safety claim | 3,350 lines. No LLVM. |\n\n\"If it compiles, it's contained\" riffs on Rust's \"if it compiles, it works\" and is simultaneously the exact mechanism: admission happens at compile time, so a successful compile *is* the containment. Sandboxes cannot say it — they build first and isolate after.\n\nLandmines when improvising around these lines: `defmacro`, `throw`/`try`/`catch`, `atom`/`ref`, host interop and `eval` are all in `lang/guest-grammar.edn` `:forbidden-heads`. Never sell Kotoba on macro power or homoiconicity — a Lisper will test it in ten seconds. \"The Lisp shape without the Lisp reach\" is the honest and stronger claim. Avoid puns on Scheme (a scheme) and Racket (a racket); both words mean something shady in English. Arc is Paul Graham's Lisp — that comparison is an interview remark, never a banner.\n\n---\n\n## Company\n\nCompany name: Kotoba\n\nDescribe what your company does in 50 characters or less:\nSafety-first language + hosting for AI code\n(43 characters. This field is a description, not the tagline — YC skims it. The company is Deno-shaped: language plus hosted deploy. Not a language-only research apply. The tagline lives in the video, the site and the deck: **If it compiles, it's contained.**)\n\nCompany URL: https://kotoba-lang.org\n\nProduct link: https://github.com/kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang\nOrgs (public, already shipped): https://github.com/kotoba-lang , https://github.com/cloud-itonami , https://github.com/net-kotobase\nSite: https://kotoba-lang.org\n\nLogin credentials: none. Public site.\n\nWhat is your company going to make?\nAI agents now write most new code, and it runs with the same authority as a trusted human: the network, the disk, the secrets, the money. Sandboxes contain a process after they have built it. They cannot tell you what the code was permitted to do, or what it actually did.\n\nKotoba makes authority a type. An effect that was not granted cannot be expressed, so the compiler refuses to emit the artifact at all — nothing has to be contained at runtime because nothing unsafe was ever built. If it compiles, it's contained. Build and run are content-addressed, so what ran, under which policy, with which grants, is a verifiable record rather than a log line: change one byte and verification fails.\n\nThe compiler ships today — WebAssembly and native x86-64/AArch64, with no LLVM and no C in the trusted path; the entire code generator is 3,350 lines of Clojure. Thirty-three of our own production inference cores compile through it. The missing paid face is hosted admission: submit code, grant capabilities, run, get a signed receipt. Our first customers are teams that already let agents write production software — we are one of them.\n\nWhere do you live now, and where would the company be based after YC?\nTokyo, Japan / San Francisco, United States\n\nExplain your decision regarding location.\nJun Kawasaki lives in Tokyo. The batch is in-person in San Francisco, so the company will be there for those three months. After the batch, SF remains the commercial base; Tokyo remains where we build. Jun will relocate for the batch. Ichihara's batch location will be on their founder profile (still incomplete).\n\nBatch preference:\nFall 2026 (late application). Winter 2027 is acceptable if review slips. Do not wait on incorporation.\n\n---\n\n## Progress\n\nHow far along are you?\nWorking compiler, published toolchain releases, and a public language site. Compiles to WebAssembly and to native x86-64 and AArch64. We dogfood it hard: 2,103 Kotoba modules across 1,256 of our repositories, including 33 production inference cores on murakumo that compile to machine code through this compiler. No public billed deploy yet. No external language customers. No revenue. The next ship is hosted admission with capability grants, not more language surface.\n\nHow long have each of you been working on this? How much of that has been full-time?\nFull-time on Kotoba and its compiler through 2026. I do not have a clean earlier start date to claim, so I am not inventing one. Sister products and the Japanese operating company are older; they are background, not this company's age.\n\nWhat tech stack are you using?\nKotoba (.kotoba source). Compiler written in portable Clojure/ClojureScript. Backends: Wasm / Wasm Component, native x86-64, native AArch64. Site on Cloudflare. AI editors: Cursor and Claude. Inference for our own tools: self-hosted murakumo-main. No JVM in the shipped language runtime.\n\nOptional coding-agent session: skip unless owner exports a real session they want partners to read.\n\nAre people using your product?\nYes, internally. No, not as paying external language users.\n\nHow many active users or customers do you have? How many are paying?\nExternal paying customers of Kotoba the language: 0. Internal production use: our own inference fleet (33 compiled cores). Public site exists; we do not have a measured external developer-user count we trust, so we are not inventing one.\n\nWhen will you have a version people can use?\nThe compiler and site are usable now. A hosted runtime with billed capability grants is the missing productized face. That is the first thing to ship if accepted.\n\nDo you have revenue?\nNo.\n\nHow much revenue? / Where does your revenue come from?\n$0. Do not list sister-product traffic or historical ad cents as Kotoba revenue.\n\nPrevious YC application / pivot:\nFirst application. No prior YC batch.\n\nIncubator / accelerator:\nNone.\n\n---\n\n## Verified evidence (measured 2026-08-17, first-hand — quote these, invent nothing else)\n\nEvery number here was executed or counted on the day, not read off a README. If a partner checks one and it holds, the rest of the application is believed.\n\n**The pipeline runs.** `amu compile --target wasm32` → 331 bytes in 1.8s, `main()` returns 42 under `WebAssembly.instantiate`. `--target aarch64` → KEXE plus provenance sidecar, 1.8s. Deny-by-default: no policy → `:kotoba/admission-denied`; with `{:allow #{[:cap/call 7]}}` → compiles. `sign-output-set` → `verify-output-set --trust` → `:publisher-authenticated true`; one byte altered → `\"output set is not committed\"`.\n\n**Kotoba in production.** 2,103 `.kotoba` modules across 1,256 repositories (of 4,581 checkouts). 1,182 of those repositories hold exactly one — the decision-core pattern, mechanism left in Clojure. 14 hold ten or more (722 modules: the toolchain itself). Baseline for comparison: 25,919 `.cljc` files across 1,864 repositories (self-measured 2026-07-18, `lang/q9-migration.edn`). Say \"2,103 Kotoba modules\", never \"thousands of Kotoba libraries\" — anyone who clones will see the ratio.\n\n**Trusted base.** Code generator = `x86_64.cljc` 1,961 lines + `aarch64.cljc` 1,389 lines = 3,350 lines. `kotoba-native` contains zero C, assembly or Rust files; foreign code exists only in platform loaders/harnesses and a benchmark kernel, outside the generation path.\n\n**Public surface.** 4,038 public repositories (kotoba-lang 2,097 / cloud-itonami 1,825 / etzhayyim 75 / gftdcojp 28 / network-awai 10 / net-kotobase 3). Toolchain v0.7.1 published 2026-08-13 for four platforms with checksums; brew tap and install.sh both resolve. Live: kotoba-lang.org, kotobase.net (+ datomic.kotobase.net), itonami.cloud, api.murakumo.cloud, and x402.nexus serving a live catalog of 7 priced endpoints.\n\n**Weaknesses to state before being asked.** Release download counts are 51 (linux-amd64, mostly CI) and 2 (darwin-arm64) — there is no external pull yet, which is exactly what the batch is for. All 11 capability kits are `:reference :implemented` with `:wasm-aot`, `:native-aot` and `:jit` still `:pending`, which is why we will not sell an effect as production yet. Our own `kotoba-lang/security` heatmap rates the core repositories Grade B, gate Partial, evidence E3 — no E4 production receipt. The aiueos OS reproducibility rebuild does not boot (ADR-0035: multiboot gate 3/3, UEFI gate fails on `#UD`); the OS is depth for the interview, never a headline claim.\n\n**Do not claim.** That Kotoba is faster or safer than Rust — our own `amu/docs/performance.md` explicitly declines the universal ranking and claims no native speedup. That Kotoba does advanced numerics — f64/f32/vector-f64 scalars exist, there is no numeric library. That the OS and browser are done. That x402 is integrated into the language — it is an adjacent live facilitator, and there is no payment capability kit. That agents wrote the code base in a way commit history proves — only 4 of the last 40 commits touching murakumo's `.kotoba` carry an agent trailer; say it in the first person instead.\n\n---\n\n## Idea\n\nWhy did you pick this idea? Domain expertise? How do you know people need it?\nI run a company where AI agents write most of the new code. The remaining failure is not syntax. It is ambient authority: generated code that can reach the network, secrets, and money because the language allowed it. Process sandboxes still contain a full OS. I built Kotoba because I needed a language that cannot express forbidden effects. The need is from our own production, not from a TAM slide. Domain expertise: at DEF CON 33 I competed as a one-person team in the Car Hacking Village CTF and placed 2nd among one-person teams and 9th overall.\n\nWho are your competitors? What do you understand that they don't?\nSandboxes — E2B, Modal, Firecracker, gVisor, Cloudflare Workers, Deno Deploy. They contain a process that still speaks JavaScript or Python with ambient authority. They build the artifact first and isolate it afterwards. We refuse to build it.\n\nUnison is the closest idea: content-addressed, with algebraic effects. But abilities are a type discipline, not a security boundary, and Unison asks a team to rewrite everything inside its own codebase manager. That is why it has been admired for nine years and adopted by almost no one.\n\nRust gives memory safety inside one program and says nothing about authority. Rust also has an `unsafe` block; Kotoba has no in-language form that suspends the rules at all — the only exception is an explicit signed permit that lives outside the program. And Rust's guarantee ends where LLVM begins: millions of lines of C++ you have to trust. Our whole code generator is 3,350 lines of Clojure.\n\nWhat we understand: adoption is incremental or it does not happen, and the trusted base has to be small enough to read. Kotoba is Clojure-shaped S-expressions — the shape LLMs write most reliably — minus eval, host interop, ambient mutable state, and macros. A team moves only its decisions and keeps its mechanism: 2,103 Kotoba modules now sit inside 1,256 of our own repositories whose implementation is still Clojure. seL4 and Capsicum are capability systems without a language agents actually write. WASM Component Model is plumbing, not a product. kotobase is our own storage kit, not a competitor. itonami is not this company.\n\nHow do or will you make money? How much could you make?\nDeno model. The language can be open source. The company sells hosted deploy. Usage customers pay per invocation and per capability grant, like Deno Deploy. Team customers pay for policy: which capabilities an agent may hold, with a signed receipt of what ran. murakumo is our first cluster (internal), not a product we are applying with. kotobase is the storage capability that deploy can grant, not a second applicant company; Terms still DRAFT, $0 verified external revenue. No Kotoba deploy revenue yet. We are not inventing ARR. We will not sell an effect (HTTP, storage, LLM) as production until that kit is qualified on the shipped backend.\n\nCategory: Developer Tools\n(Secondary if the form allows only one: Security.)\n\nOther ideas considered:\nWe are applying with one product: Kotoba language plus hosted deploy. murakumo is not another idea. It is the fleet we already deploy onto (33 compiled cores, internal). kotobase.net is a content-addressed GraphDB. In this company it is the storage capability the runtime can grant, not a separate YC product. Live site, Terms DRAFT, $0 verified external revenue. cloud-itonami is an industry operating system. Later it can be a distribution channel into real businesses. It is not this application. Live site, $0 verified external payment. If partners think the database or the industry OS is the better company, that is useful signal. The main answer is still language plus deploy.\n\n---\n\n## Equity\n\nHave you formed ANY legal entity yet?\nYes — related companies exist. The YC applicant company (Kotoba) is not formed yet.\n\nPlease list all legal entities:\n1. AWAI Network, L.L.C. — Delaware LLC, formed 2026-07-20 via Stripe Atlas, Delaware file 10704996. Sister operator for other products. Not the YC company.\n2. Gftd Japan K.K. — Japan, Corporate Number 1011101086505. Employment and some collection-agent work. Not the YC company.\n\nKotoba / Kotoba Lang, Inc. (planned Delaware C-Corp) will be formed after this application. Language, compiler, and runtime IP are intended to live there.\n\nDescribe the breakdown of equity ownership among the founders, employees, and any other shareholders.\nApplicant company not formed. Planned founder split once the C-Corp exists: Jun Kawasaki 90%, Ichihara 10%. No employees with equity. Sister companies are separate cap tables and are not this company.\n\nHave you taken any investment yet?\nNo, not for Kotoba.\n\nHow much money have you raised in total (USD)?\n0\n\nAre you currently fundraising?\nApplying to YC. Not otherwise fundraising.\n\n---\n\n## Curious\n\nWhat convinced you to apply to Y Combinator?\nThe compiler already runs our own production cores, and we still do not have a company a US buyer can write a deploy check to. YC is the shortest path to one company, one product (language plus hosted deploy), and a Delaware C-Corp that can take a SAFE. I am not applying because a market slide said to, and I am not applying with a pile of sister sites.\n\nHow did you hear about Y Combinator?\nLong-standing. OWNER-FILL if they want a first-contact story (HN, a specific batch company, an event).\n\n---\n\n## Owner remaining (do not skip)\n\n1. Record the 1-minute founder video. Upload unlisted YouTube. Paste URL.\n2. Fill founder profile: phone, education, LinkedIn, personal email.\n3. Write the non-computer systems hack in your own voice.\n4. Create/login at https://www.ycombinator.com/apply and paste.\n5. Form the Delaware C-Corp later. Do not delay submit for that.\n6. Ichihara must complete the founder profile at i@awai.network (submit is blocked until then). Known facts now filled: university senpai, 15+ years, company together at Jun age 20, Jun 90% / Ichihara 10%, Ichihara = marketing/sales/bizdev. Still needed: Ichihara profile, 1-minute video of both founders, Ichihara batch location, non-computer hack.\n\nDo not: claim sister-product users as Kotoba customers; claim $0 as unknown; use AWAI LLC as the YC company name; wait for counsel before submitting the application (incorporation is post-accept).\n"}]
